.TH "zxc" "1" "" "zxc" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
\f[B]zxc\f[R], \f[B]unzxc\f[R], \f[B]zxccat\f[R] \- Compress or
decompress .zxc files
.SH SYNOPSIS
\f[B]zxc\f[R] [\f[I]OPTIONS\f[R]] [\f[I]INPUT\-FILE\f[R]]
[\f[I]OUTPUT\-FILE\f[R]]
.PP
\f[B]unzxc\f[R] is equivalent to \f[B]zxc \-d\f[R].
.PP
\f[B]zxccat\f[R] is equivalent to \f[B]zxc \-dc\f[R] applied to every
\f[I]INPUT\-FILE\f[R] in turn, writing them all to standard output.
.PP
\f[B]unzxc\f[R] and \f[B]zxccat\f[R] are symlinks to \f[B]zxc\f[R],
installed on POSIX systems only.
.SH DESCRIPTION
\f[B]zxc\f[R] is a command\-line interface for the ZXC compression
library, a high\-performance lossless compression algorithm optimized
for maximum decompression throughput.
.PP
\f[B]zxc\f[R] is designed for the \f[I]\(dqWrite Once, Read
Many\(dq\f[R] paradigm.
It trades compression speed to generate a bitstream specifically
structured to maximize decompression speed, effectively offloading
complexity from the decoder to the encoder.
It aims to provide very high decompression speeds across modern
architectures while maintaining competitive compression ratios.
ZXC is particularly suited for scenarios such as Game Assets, Firmware
or App Bundles where data is compressed once on a build server and
decompressed millions of times on user devices.
.PP
By default, \f[B]zxc\f[R] compresses a single \f[I]INPUT\-FILE\f[R].
If no \f[I]OUTPUT\-FILE\f[R] is provided, \f[B]zxc\f[R] will
automatically append the \f[CR].zxc\f[R] extension to the input
filename.
If no \f[I]INPUT\-FILE\f[R] is provided, \f[B]zxc\f[R] will read from
standard input (\f[CR]stdin\f[R]) and write to standard output
(\f[CR]stdout\f[R]).
.SH STANDARD MODES
.TP
\f[B]\-z\f[R], \f[B]\-\-compress\f[R]
Compress FILE.
This is the default mode if no mode is specified.
.TP
\f[B]\-d\f[R], \f[B]\-\-decompress\f[R]
Decompress FILE.
This is the default mode when \f[B]zxc\f[R] is invoked under the name
\f[B]unzxc\f[R] (typically an installed symlink).
An explicit mode flag still takes precedence.
.TP
\f[B]\-l\f[R], \f[B]\-\-list\f[R]
List archive information: compressed size, uncompressed size,
compression ratio, checksum and seek table (\f[CR]enabled\f[R],
\f[CR]disabled\f[R], or \f[CR]mixed\f[R] across concatenated frames),
dictionary ID (if any) and frame count; the file name is shown when
several files are listed.
Also accepts a \f[CR].zxd\f[R] dictionary file, in which case it prints
the dictionary\(aqs \f[CR]dict_id\f[R].
.TP
\f[B]\-\-train\f[R]
Train a dictionary from the input \f[I]FILE\f[R]s given as training
samples.
The output path is set with \f[B]\-o\f[R] (see \f[B]\-\-output\f[R]);
when \f[B]\-o\f[R] is omitted, the dictionary is written to
\f[CR]dictionary_<dict_id>.zxd\f[R] in the current directory.
See \f[B]DICTIONARIES\f[R].
.TP
\f[B]\-t\f[R], \f[B]\-\-test\f[R]
Test the integrity of a compressed FILE.
It decodes the file and verifies its checksum (if present) without
writing any output.
Checksum status reflects the archive header.
With \f[B]\-t\f[R], three outcomes are reported: verified, none present,
or skipped via \f[B]\-N\f[R].
Input streamed from a pipe reports unknown, since its header cannot be
re\-read.
All four cases exit 0 on a valid archive.
Under \f[B]\-j\f[R], \f[CR]checksum_verified\f[R] is true only when a
checksum was actually checked.
\f[CR]checksum\f[R] reports \f[CR]enabled\f[R], \f[CR]disabled\f[R],
\f[CR]mixed\f[R] (concatenated frames that differ), or
\f[CR]unknown\f[R] (pipe input), the same values as \f[B]\-l\f[R].
.TP
\f[B]\-b\f[R], \f[B]\-\-bench\f[R] [\f[I]N\f[R]]
Benchmark in\-memory performance.
Loads the input file entirely into RAM and measures raw algorithm
throughput (default duration is 5 seconds).
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\f[B]\-m\f[R], \f[B]\-\-multiple\f[R]
Process multiple files at once.
When specified, all subsequent non\-option arguments are treated as
input files.
For each input file, a corresponding \f[CR].zxc\f[R] file is created (or
decompressed into its original name).
Output cannot be written to standard output (\f[CR]stdout\f[R]) when
this mode is enabled.
.TP
\f[B]\-r\f[R], \f[B]\-\-recursive\f[R]
Recursively process directories.
When specified, any directory listed as an argument will be traversed,
and all regular files within it will be processed (compressed or
decompressed).
This option implicitly enables \f[CR]\-\-multiple\f[R] mode.
.TP
\f[B]\-1\f[R]..\f[B]\-7\f[R]
Set the compression level, from 1 (fastest compression) to 7 (smallest
output).
Default: 3.
Higher levels trade compression speed for ratio; decompression stays
fast at every level.
Levels 6 (density) and 7 (ultra) compress far more slowly and suit data
written once and read many times.
.TP
\f[B]\-T\f[R], \f[B]\-\-threads\f[R] \f[I]N\f[R]
Set the number of threads to use for compression and decompression.
A value of \f[CR]0\f[R] means auto\-detection based on the number of
available CPU cores.
.TP
\f[B]\-B\f[R], \f[B]\-\-block\-size\f[R] \f[I]SIZE\f[R]
Set the compression block size.
\f[I]SIZE\f[R] must be a power of two between \f[B]4K\f[R] and
\f[B]2M\f[R] (e.g.
\f[CR]4K\f[R], \f[CR]64K\f[R], \f[CR]256K\f[R], \f[CR]512K\f[R],
\f[CR]1M\f[R]).
Smaller blocks reduce memory usage and improve random\-access
decompression; larger blocks generally yield better compression ratios.
The default is \f[B]512K\f[R], tuned for bulk/archival workloads where
ratio and decompression throughput matter most.
This option is only meaningful during compression; the block size is
stored in the archive header and automatically used during
decompression.
.TP
\f[B]\-C\f[R], \f[B]\-\-checksum\f[R]
Enable block hashing during compression using the rapidhash algorithm.
Recommended for data integrity validation.
Checksum verification is automatically performed during extraction when
enabled.
.TP
\f[B]\-N\f[R], \f[B]\-\-no\-checksum\f[R]
Explicitly disable checksum generation.
.TP
\f[B]\-D\f[R], \f[B]\-\-dict\f[R] \f[I]FILE\f[R]
Use a pre\-trained dictionary (\f[CR].zxd\f[R]) for compression or
decompression.
On compression, the archive records the dictionary\(aqs
\f[CR]dict_id\f[R] in its header.
On decompression, \f[B]\-D\f[R] is \f[B]required\f[R] for any archive
that was compressed with a dictionary \(em there is no auto\-lookup;
without it, decompression fails with a dictionary\-required error (see
\f[B]DICTIONARIES\f[R]).
.TP
\f[B]\-o\f[R], \f[B]\-\-output\f[R] \f[I]FILE\f[R]
Write output to \f[I]FILE\f[R].
For compression/decompression this overrides the positional
\f[I]OUTPUT\-FILE\f[R] (single\-file mode only); when omitted the output
name is derived from the input.
For \f[B]\-\-train\f[R] it sets the dictionary path: if \f[I]FILE\f[R]
is a directory (or ends with a path separator) the dictionary is saved
inside it as \f[CR]dictionary_<dict_id>.zxd\f[R], otherwise
\f[I]FILE\f[R] is used verbatim; when omitted the dictionary is written
to \f[CR]dictionary_<dict_id>.zxd\f[R] in the current directory.
.TP
\f[B]\-S\f[R], \f[B]\-\-seekable\f[R]
Append a seek table to the archive during compression.
This transforms the file into a random\-access format (Seekable
Archive), allowing the decoder to instantly locate and decompress
specific blocks in \f[CR]O(1)\f[R] time without reading the entire file.
Ideal for compressed filesystems, game assets, and log analysis.
.TP
\f[B]\-k\f[R], \f[B]\-\-keep\f[R]
Keep the input file after an in\-place compression or decompression.
By default the input is removed \f[B]only\f[R] when its output name is
auto\-derived (e.g.
\f[CR]file\f[R] → \f[CR]file.zxc\f[R]).
When the output is named explicitly \(em with \f[B]\-o\f[R] or a
positional \f[I]OUTPUT\-FILE\f[R] \(em the input is always kept, so this
flag is only needed for the in\-place case.
.TP
\f[B]\-f\f[R], \f[B]\-\-force\f[R]
Force overwrite of the \f[I]OUTPUT\-FILE\f[R] if it already exists.
.TP
\f[B]\-c\f[R], \f[B]\-\-stdout\f[R]
Force writing to standard output (\f[CR]stdout\f[R]), even if it is the
console.
.TP
\f[B]\-v\f[R], \f[B]\-\-verbose\f[R]
Enable verbose logging mode.
Outputs more detailed information during operations.
.TP
\f[B]\-q\f[R], \f[B]\-\-quiet\f[R]
Enable quiet mode, suppressing all non\-error output (such as progress
bars or real\-time statistics).
.TP
\f[B]\-j\f[R], \f[B]\-\-json\f[R]
Output results in JSON format.
This is particularly useful for scripting, benchmarking, and the
\f[CR]\-\-list\f[R] mode.
.TP
\f[B]\-\-progress\f[R] \f[I]MODE\f[R]
Control the progress display: \f[B]auto\f[R] (default) shows a progress
bar on the standard error stream when it is a terminal and the file is
larger than 1 MB; \f[B]always\f[R] forces progress reporting even when
standard error is not a terminal (updates are then printed one per line,
at most once per second) or when the size is unknown (stdin input);
\f[B]never\f[R] disables it.
\f[B]\-q\f[R] suppresses progress regardless of this option.
.SH SPECIAL OPTIONS
.TP
\f[B]\-V\f[R], \f[B]\-\-version\f[R]
Display the version of the zxc library and the compiled architecture
information, then exit.
.TP
\f[B]\-h\f[R], \f[B]\-\-help\f[R]
Display a help message and exit.
.SH DICTIONARIES
For workloads compressed in small blocks (4K\(en128K), a pre\-trained
dictionary can dramatically improve the compression ratio.
The dictionary prefills the LZ77 window at the start of every block, so
the benefit is per\-block: the smaller the block, the more it relies on
the dictionary for early matches.
.PP
Dictionaries are external \f[CR].zxd\f[R] files referenced from the
archive header by a 32\-bit \f[CR]dict_id\f[R] (a hash of the dictionary
content).
The \f[CR].zxd\f[R] extension is cosmetic; a \f[CR].zxd\f[R] file is
identified by its magic word, not its name.
.PP
When \f[B]\-\-train\f[R]\(aqs \f[B]\-o\f[R] targets a directory (or is
omitted, defaulting to the current directory), \f[B]zxc\f[R] names the
file \f[CR]dictionary_<dict_id>.zxd\f[R] (the \f[CR]dict_id\f[R] is the
lowercase 8\-digit hex reported by \f[CR]zxc \-l\f[R]).
On decompression the dictionary is \f[B]not\f[R] auto\-located: an
archive compressed with a dictionary must be decompressed by supplying
that dictionary with \f[B]\-D\f[R], otherwise decompression fails with a
dictionary\-required error.
.PP
If no matching dictionary can be found (or supplied), decompression
fails with a dictionary\-required error rather than producing corrupt
output.
.SH CONCATENATED ARCHIVES
Archives can be concatenated (\f[CR]cat a.zxc b.zxc > ab.zxc\f[R]):
\f[B]\-d\f[R] decodes them one after the other into one output, and
\f[B]\-t\f[R] checks every one.
Each keeps its own block size, checksum, seek table and dictionary;
\f[B]\-l\f[R] reports the frame count and \f[CR]mixed\f[R] where they
differ; with \f[B]\-v\f[R] or \f[B]\-j\f[R] it lists every block size
and dictionary ID instead.
\f[B]\-D\f[R] supplies one dictionary: frames compressed with another
fail with a dictionary\-mismatch error.
.SH EXAMPLES
\f[B]Compress a file:\f[R] zxc data.txt
.PP
\f[B]Compress a file with high density (Level 6, archival):\f[R] zxc \-6
data.bin
.PP
\f[B]Compress a file with maximum density (Level 7, Ultra):\f[R] zxc \-7
data.bin
.PP
\f[B]Decompress a file:\f[R] zxc \-d data.txt.zxc
.PP
\f[B]Decompress a file using the unzxc alias:\f[R] unzxc data.txt.zxc
.PP
\f[B]Print the contents of several archives, one after the other:\f[R]
zxccat part1.zxc part2.zxc | grep ERROR
.PP
\f[B]Compress multiple files independently:\f[R] zxc \-m file1.txt
file2.txt file3.txt
.PP
\f[B]Compress all files in a directory recursively:\f[R] zxc \-r
\&./my_folder
.PP
\f[B]Decompress all files in a directory recursively:\f[R] zxc \-d \-r
\&./my_folder
.PP
\f[B]Decompress a file to standard output:\f[R] zxc \-dc data.txt.zxc >
data.txt
.PP
\f[B]List archive information:\f[R] zxc \-l data.txt.zxc
.PP
\f[B]Compress with a custom block size (64 KB):\f[R] zxc \-B 64K
data.bin data.zxc
.PP
\f[B]Compress with maximum block size (2 MB):\f[R] zxc \-6 \-B 2M
data.bin data.zxc
.PP
\f[B]Run a benchmark for 10 seconds:\f[R] zxc \-b 10 data.txt
.PP
\f[B]Train a dictionary into a directory (saved as
\f[CB]dictionary_<dict_id>.zxd\f[B]):\f[R] zxc \-\-train \-o dicts/
samples/*.json
.PP
\f[B]Compress with a dictionary using small blocks:\f[R] zxc \-B 4K \-D
dicts/dictionary_bc46eec1.zxd input.json
.PP
\f[B]Decompress (the dictionary is required, pass it with \-D):\f[R] zxc
\-d \-D dicts/dictionary_bc46eec1.zxd input.json.zxc
